WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.593 --> 00:00:02.760
TIM HOCKIN: When you see
somebody work on Kubernetes

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.010
for the first time and they
realize what it's actually

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.500
capable of doing, and they
get this spark of like,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.540
wow, that was really amazing.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.130
That was incredible, I
didn't know I could do that.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.010
That moment is what
keeps me going.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.260
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Kubernetes
is a container management

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:16.810
platform.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:17.610
It's open source.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.110
If you have a
collection of machines

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.060
you can package up your
applications and containers

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.020
and give it to Kubernetes
and it'll run it for you.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.145
CHEN GOLDBERG: What it
gives developers is actually

00:00:25.145 --> 00:00:27.630
the luxury of not caring
about the infrastructure.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.330
Kubernetes on its own is
taking care of monitoring,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.520
logging, scaling,
and other things that

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.950
are required in other
managing applications,

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.720
in productions, et cetera.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.740
APARNA SINHA: Kubernetes,
the word stands for helmsman.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.630
Kubernetes is that
system that makes sure

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.160
that your applications
are always

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.700
afloat and always accessible
in a sea of resources

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.167
that is the cloud.

00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:51.250
TIM HOCKIN: It was really
critical, in my opinion,

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.380
to open source Kubernetes.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.610
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So
Google started the project

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.610
based on what we knew about how
things worked inside of Google.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.375
But having contributors
like Red Hat and others

00:00:59.375 --> 00:01:00.750
allowed us to
actually figure out

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.751
how to make it work
for the outside world.

00:01:02.751 --> 00:01:04.709
And that was the key to
success for Kubernetes.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:07.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.590
CHEN GOLDBERG: We really needed
to think about the adoption.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.030
What the customers will
know, or the users.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.940
So, originally, we really
were focusing on ease of use.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.440
And documentation
and understanding,

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.360
like, what is their
familiarity in order

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.660
for them to use the technology.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.243
BRIAN GRANT: About
six months of work,

00:01:21.243 --> 00:01:23.995
we delivered something of
value to external customers.

00:01:23.995 --> 00:01:25.370
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
GKE is probably

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:28.810
the best production-ready
Kubernetes install you can get.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.200
You click a single button.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.742
In about five minutes,
we'll give you

00:01:31.742 --> 00:01:34.315
this production ready cluster
with full lifecycle management.

00:01:34.315 --> 00:01:36.690
So what we're trying to do is
take GKE, and package it up

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.730
in Anthos, and give
you the ability

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:42.017
to do the same thing on Prem
and even other cloud providers.

00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:44.100
LAURENT GABET: We moved
from a stand up processing

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.800
to a microservices processing.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.570
And without Kubernetes it
there was no way to do that.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.050
It's an open source so
it was important for us

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.880
not to be glued in a provider.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.100
So now we're on Premise.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.440
We are deploying
Kubernetes ourselves.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.835
And on GCP we are using GKE.

00:02:00.835 --> 00:02:02.460
APARNA SINHA: Kubernetes
is everywhere.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.133
It's being used by your bank,
your retailer, your pizza shop.

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:07.800
CHUCK RHOADES: Pizza
Hut uses Kubernetes

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.670
to run a large portion of
our e-commerce services

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:12.610
and infrastructure.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:15.600
It's extremely important
for us that our services

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.930
be available when our customers
want to purchase food from us.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.850
Kubernetes has
impacted our business

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.800
by giving us just a really
great, fast speed to market

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.210
on delivering technology.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:27.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.620
BRANDON JUNG: We made the
migration from Azure to GCP.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.220
We did it for a
couple major reasons.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.963
First off the quality of
the engineering on GCP.

00:02:35.963 --> 00:02:38.130
The second piece that was
really big was Kubernetes.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.560
We are a development company.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.440
We're all about helping
people write the next thing

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:44.820
they want to build.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.233
And that's going to
be done in Kubernetes.

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:48.900
CHEN GOLDBERG: The
future of Kubernetes,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.970
it's very similar to
what infrastructure are

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.770
doing for Google developers.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.900
We want to empower
developers everywhere

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.770
to innovate without needing
to care about infrastructure

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.833
or problems that we
already solved for them.

00:02:59.833 --> 00:03:02.250
And we started seeing some
sparks of those kind of things,

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.230
really amazing
innovation of what people

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:05.850
are using Kubernetes for.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.460
And I expect we'll see
more and more amazing ideas

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.377
and how software is
really changing the world.

00:03:10.377 --> 00:03:11.752
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
I can't believe

00:03:11.752 --> 00:03:14.310
I'm talking to software, but
happy anniversary, Kubernetes.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.560
TIM HOCKIN: Happy birthday
to Kubernetes, the project,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.470
and the community.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:20.610
And thank you so much to the
thousands of people who make

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.427
my life very, very exciting.

00:03:22.427 --> 00:03:24.510
APARNA SINHA: Happy fifth
anniversary, Kubernetes.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.770
You continue to
inspire and amaze.

